# GCP — egress this entry needs.
#
# Allowlist syntax, so what you read here is what lands in the deployment's
# /etc/agent-allowlist. Uncommented lines are what the provider needs to work;
# anything below the OPTIONAL marker is off until you turn it on.
#
# METHODS is not optional in practice. Omitting it defaults the entry to
# GET,HEAD,OPTIONS, which denies every write and — see below — the token
# exchange every Google client library performs before its first call.

*.googleapis.com        GET,POST,PUT,PATCH,DELETE
sts.googleapis.com      POST
oauth2.googleapis.com   POST

# The last two lines are not redundant, in either direction.
#
# They are narrower on purpose: hostmatch.find() resolves exact before wildcard
# regardless of line order, so these two hosts get POST only while the rest of
# the googleapis surface keeps the full set.
#
# And they must be present at all, which is the non-obvious part. 040_gcp.py
# answers the token exchange at these two hosts locally, with the inert
# `proxy-injected` value, so nothing ever reaches Google — but 001_allowlist.py
# runs FIRST (001 before 040). A destination the allowlist denies never gets as
# far as the addon that would have answered it, so leaving these out breaks the
# credential chain of every Google client library, before any real API call.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — nothing below is needed for the provider to work.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# *.googleusercontent.com   GET     # signed-URL downloads some APIs redirect to
# accounts.google.com       *       # interactive sign-in; a lab should not need it
